      <memberdef kind="function" id="swim__ev_8h_1aca0ef3f080e72a1f13bd2ceca7bb96b4" prot="public" static="no" const="no" explicit="no" inline="no" virt="non-virtual">
        <type>struct ev_loop *</type>
        <definition>struct ev_loop* swim_loop</definition>
        <argsstring>(void)</argsstring>
        <name>swim_loop</name>
        <param>
          <type>void</type>
        </param>
        <briefdescription>
<para>The unit tests code with the fake events and time does lots of forbidden things: it manually invokes pending watcher callbacks; manages global time without a kernel; puts not existing descriptors into the loop. </para>        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>All these actions does not affect the loop until yield. On yield a scheduler fiber wakes up and 1) infinitely generates EV_READ on not existing descriptors because considers them closed; 2) manual pending callbacks invocation asserts, because it is not allowed for non-scheduler fibers. To avoid these problems a new isolated loop is created, not visible for the scheduler. Here the fake events library can rack and ruin whatever it wants. This function is supposed to be an alias for &apos;loop()&apos; in the Tarantool core, but be an isolated object in tests. </para>        </detaileddescription>
        <inbodydescription>
        </inbodydescription>
